  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Sudden inability to move left arm (acute left arm paralysis), noted on waking this morning (~1 hour prior to call).\n- Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating/alleviating):\n  - Onset: sudden on awakening about 1 hour before the call.\n  - Duration: ongoing at time of call (≈1 hour).\n  - Severity: complete inability to move left arm; speech described as slower/harder; some difficulty swallowing; tingling on the left side of the face.\n  - Aggravating/alleviating factors: none identified; no positional cause from sleep, no prior similar episodes.\n  - Associated symptoms: facial tingling on left side, dysarthria/slowed speech, dysphagia (difficulty swallowing). No leg symptoms. No pain, no discoloration, hand not cold.\n- Past medical history: Type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension.\n- Medications / allergies: Metformin, losartan, amlodipine. Allergy: penicillin.\n- Family / social history: Father with heart disease. Patient is an ex-smoker. Drinks alcohol (few beers at weekends, few during week). Lives alone.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: Telephone consult — no measured vital signs. On phone: no reported facial asymmetry, arm is plegic (patient-reported), left facial tingling, speech difficulty and swallowing difficulty. No limb discoloration or coldness.\n- Labs / imaging: None reported; ambulance called for emergency hospital assessment and likely stroke workup.\n- Other contextual details: Age/date of birth given (12 Aug 1958). Ambulance en route; patient advised to wait by front door.\n",
